T1-31 — Blocked: `K_conv_n` does not reach the live pointer call sites. `prose_token_send`, `prose_token_recv_classified`, and `prose_token_burn_id` accept only `ScopeInput` and call `derive_scope_primitives`, which derives the detection/MAC material from `prose_token_salt(&scope)` (public scope data). The live shipping callers in `apps/osl-hub/src/broker.rs` likewise supply only a scope and encrypted wire; no ratchet epoch secret or `K_detect` is available. `osl-ratchet-next::Session` keeps its root/chain secrets private and exposes no epoch-secret derivation for these paths. Implementing `K_detect = HKDF(K_conv_n, "osl/detect/v1")` here would require a ratchet-owned API plus propagation through the shipping send/receive boundary, outside T1-31's owned files; substituting public scope data would preserve D2-DEFECT and fail T1-T31.
T1-80 — Blocked: the live shipping send path still calls `prose_token_send` with public `ScopeInput`, so it cannot produce the secret-keyed pointer required by T1-31; the real-store proof would exercise the known D2 defect. The required T6-R7/T6-R5 eager-fetch/ACK implementation and a declared staging endpoint are also absent from this checkout.
T1-81 — Blocked: `cipher-store-cf` receives legacy scope-derived fetch authority from the live IPC path, while T1-31's secret detector and T1-30's seed-derived request authority are not shipping. A server-observability test would correctly fail until those prerequisites are wired, not prove unlinkability.
T1-82 — Blocked: no `T6-R7` eager-fetch implementation is present in the shipping app and no real-machine/Windows VM test channel is available in this Linux worktree. Writing the requested offline readability proof now would test lazy or absent behaviour, not D29/D30.
T1-84 — Blocked: the required T1-81 server-observability proof is blocked and the prerequisite T1-72/T1-74 transport and Tor evidence is not present. The allowlist cannot honestly promote claims before those proofs exist; D109-D112 do not change that dependency.
